          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- *Spatial distribution of W1.* The very first rows of W1 (image row 0, columns ~4–11 and ~17–22) already contain large, spatially coherent weights (e.g. unit 9: 0.51, 0.47 at row 0 cols 6–7; unit 4: −0.48, −0.36 at the same pixels; unit 7: 0.42, 0.36 at cols 10–11). The same is true at the bottom edge (image rows 25–27: unit 10 = 0.43/0.52, unit 7 = −0.39/−0.51, unit 15 = 0.43/0.46 at cols 7–10). Neighbouring pixels have similar values, so these are learned features, not leftover initialisation noise. By contrast the first ~3 and last ~3 *columns* of each row carry only small weights. So the informative region spans the full image height but has horizontal margins.\n- *Hidden-unit structure.* Several units show vertical stripe patterns running through many rows: units 2, 13 and 14 have a consistent negative band at columns ~6–9 from roughly row 3 down to row 20, while unit 3/5/15 have positive bands a few columns further in; unit 0 and unit 10 have strong negative lobes near the top at cols 9–11 and 17–19 (row 1–2), which looks like the two sides of a garment's neck/shoulder region. Unit 15 is strongly positive in the lower-left and lower-right quadrants (rows 21–27), i.e. it responds to mass at the bottom of the frame. Unit 10 and unit 2 have a strong positive patch at the very bottom (rows 26–27, cols 4–8) with unit 7 strongly negative there — a bottom-edge detector. Units 5 and 14 have large positive biases (0.58, 0.65), consistent with inputs that have many non-zero (grey) pixels.\n- *Output layer.* W2 shows class groupings: units 1, 8 and 10 are positive for both class 0 and class 6; unit 0, 7 and 9 are positive for both 7 and 9 while unit 5 (0.24 vs −0.93) and unit 11 (−0.78 vs 0.57) split them; classes 2 and 4 share units 2/3/13 with the same sign. So the confusable pairs are (0,6), (2,4), (7,9), with 5 loosely attached to 7/9.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\nFor MNIST, KMNIST or EMNIST the digits/characters are size-normalised into a 20×20 box centred in the 28×28 frame, so the outer rows and columns are always zero and their weights would remain small and random. Here the top and bottom rows carry some of the largest, most coherent weights in the whole network, which rules those datasets out. The pattern \"full vertical extent, horizontal margins, vertical stripe detectors, bottom-of-frame detectors, shared units between specific class pairs\" fits Fashion-MNIST: tops, dresses, trousers and coats reach the top and bottom edges, footwear lies in the lower-middle band, the long vertical edge detectors correspond to sleeve/body/trouser-leg boundaries, and the W2 groupings match the known confusions T-shirt↔Shirt (0,6), Pullover↔Coat (2,4), Sneaker↔Ankle boot (7,9), with Sandal (5) near the other footwear.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando 28×28 greyscale clothing images).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 80%",
